# Brand Guidelines for Frontend Development

When working on frontend components and UI improvements, always reference and follow the brand guidelines from [docs/brand_book.md](mdc:docs/brand_book.md).

## Key Brand Elements to Apply

### Color Palette
- **Signal Green** (`#00A86B`) - Primary UI, links, CTAs
- **Audible Gold** (`#FFC300`) - Buttons, KPI highlights
- **Turf Dark Green** (`#016140`) - Dark-mode panels, hover states
- **Gridiron Graphite** (`#1E1E1E`) - Text, shadow overlays
- **White Space** (`#FFFFFF`) - Background, negative space

### Typography
- **Primary**: Inter (variable; weight 300-700) for dashboards and mobile readability
- **Secondary**: Space Grotesk for headlines and numeric data
- **Code/Data**: IBM Plex Mono for snippets and API examples

### UX Principles
- **Insight at a glance** - default screen should show live ADP delta heat map
- **One-hand mobile use** - thumb-zone compliant controls
- **Accessibility First** - all interactive elements ≥ 44px; haptic feedback on tap; color-blind safe palette

### Voice & Tone
- **Authoritative but never condescending** - think Patrick Mahomes explaining a scatter-plot
- **Conversational** - use contractions, rhetorical questions, vivid verbs
- **Playful when appropriate** - puns okay in headlines but reserve analysis sections for straight talk

### Design System Tokens
- `--radius-card`: 12px
- `--space-m`: 16px
- `--elev-card`: 4px shadow

## Implementation Checklist
- [ ] Use brand colors for all UI elements
- [ ] Apply proper typography hierarchy
- [ ] Ensure mobile-first responsive design
- [ ] Meet WCAG 2.2 AA contrast requirements
- [ ] Follow the "clarity over clutter" principle
- [ ] Design for both spreadsheet ninjas and casual phone drafters

Reference the full brand book for complete guidelines on voice, visuals, and experience patterns.
